<urn:atm:triage:finding/RSH-013>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "RSH-013" ;
  triage:title "Windows polling thread early-exit returns without notifying condvar — graceful-shutdown livelock (lifecycle_control.rs:266)" ;
  triage:description "crates/atm-daemon/src/lifecycle_control.rs:266 — The RSH-012 fix added `if terminate.load(Ordering::SeqCst) { return; }` as an early-exit guard at the top of the Windows polling loop body. However this `return` exits the thread without calling `state_change.notify()`. When SIGINT/SIGTERM arrives while the thread is sleeping (line 279), signal_hook sets `terminate=true`. On the next iteration line 266 fires `return` immediately, bypassing the `state_change.notify()` call at line 274. Any caller blocked in `wait_for_state_change` (e.g., the daemon main loop) is never woken, leaving the daemon hung indefinitely on shutdown even though the OS-level signal was delivered. Fix: call `state_change.notify()` before returning in the early-exit guard: `if terminate.load(Ordering::SeqCst) { let _ = state_change.notify(); return; }`. Alternatively restructure the loop so the change-detection block at lines 271-275 is evaluated before the early-exit, eliminating the redundant third read and ensuring notification on all terminate transitions." ;
  triage:category "RSH" ;
  triage:severity "critical" ;
  triage:triageMode "followup_pass" ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-S" ;
  triage:repeatable false ;
  triage:sweepScope "file" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady true ;
  triage:triagedAt "2026-05-09T20:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
  triage:hasOccurrence <urn:atm:triage:occurrence/RSH-013/s4/1> ;
